{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And Cain said to Abel his brother.</b> Scripture does not explain what he said to him. It appears that one may explain this saying from the language of ״יִתְאַמְּרוּ כָּל פּוֹעֲלֵי אָוֶן״ (Psalms 94:4), which is the language of boasting, and likewise ״אֶת ה׳ הֶאֱמַרְתָּ״ (Deuteronomy 27:17). What it means is that Cain boasted over him, saying: 'Mine is the world and its fullness, and the land on which you stand is mine.' Immediately, ״וַיָּקָם עָלָיו״ — over the matter of the field, and killed him. And above I explained the matter of the field — that is, that he said to him: 'When you were in the field, a place where people are not to be found there, then you would never have roused yourself to any deed on your own, for this offering that you brought — from me you saw it and so you did.' And according to this, the interpretation of this verse ״וַיֹּאמֶר קַיִן״ will be that Cain boasted, saying: 'I am better than you, for I brought an offering first, and from me the act of offering was drawn to you.' And this is what it says ״אֶל הֶבֶל אָחִיו״ — for he said: 'I was the cause toward you, for had you not seen that I brought an offering, you would not have brought any at all.'"], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:27:00.900138535Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}